  "recommended_action": "Correction to my last step: T-1 is not on a straight run at Z-HQ. It is circling over open land about 800 m or more from Z-HOUSE, so it is not about to reach a critical asset. I keep HIGH on the number of indicators. (1) Rung 2 now: warn, and notify base security and the control room because the pilot is unknown (ROE-6.5). Watch the drone's return path for the pilot. If the pilot is found on base, send security (ROE-6.1). If off base on U.S. land, call Harborview Police (ROE-6.2, N-17). If on the water, call the Coast Guard (ROE-6.3, N-18). Keep S-GIM on T-1 to hold PVID. (2) Rung 3: the RF jammer is the lowest rung likely to work, because T-1 has a live control link (ROE-5.1) and is within jammer geometry. Use it only with operator plus control-room approval (ROE-4.1). Before use, the control room must confirm with the tower that no crewed aircraft is within 3 km of the target (ROE-5.2). N-12 says Z-APPR is used only when the tower reports traffic, and the data shows no aircraft positions. Plan for link loss: the drone may hover, land, return, or continue on its own (ROE-5.3). Pair the jam with a pilot search. Because the drone is not closing on an asset, the control room can reasonably hold at rung 2 while security searches, and jam if the track turns toward Z-HQ, Z-HOUSE, Z-FUEL, or Z-AIR. If it does, the threat becomes CRITICAL and the control room re-plans (ROE-3.1). The interceptor is next only if the jammer fails or is blocked."
